\pard\qc\b\sl480\slmult1 Movie Recommender System: Project Documentation\b0\par
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Project Overview\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 The Movie Recommender System, presented as Reelwise, helps users discover films related to a title they already enjoy. Users select a movie, optionally filter by genre, and choose the number of suggestions. A second view supports catalog search and browsing. The app demonstrates a recommendation pipeline; it is not a production streaming service.
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 Recommendations compare the selected film with other catalog entries using descriptive metadata. The app does not collect ratings, track viewing history, build a personal profile, or learn from interactions. Its "For you" label means suggestions based on the selected title, not individualized predictions.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Project Goals and Users\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 The goal is a transparent, easy-to-run example of movie discovery. Users can start from a familiar film, see ranked alternatives, or search by title, performer, director, plot, or genre. Local data avoids accounts and a remote database, making the project useful for learning and small demonstrations.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b User Interface\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 Built with Streamlit, Reelwise has recommendation and catalog tabs. Select boxes choose a seed title and genre; a slider sets result count. Cards show title, year, genres, poster, and similarity. Search covers title, director, cast, plot, and genre. Poster images load from TMDB's image CDN and require internet access, while movie records are local (Streamlit, n.d.).
\page
\pard\qc\b\sl480\slmult1 Method and Data\b0\par
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Catalog Structure\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 The local movies.csv contains 22 records: title, year, pipe-separated genres, director, cast, plot overview, and poster URL. The app reads the file with pandas and caches it during a Streamlit session. This hand-curated sample keeps the project self-contained but is not comprehensive or regularly updated.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Feature Preparation\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 Each movie becomes a text string containing genres, director, cast, and overview. Genres are repeated three times and director twice to increase their influence; cast and overview appear once. scikit-learn's TfidfVectorizer converts these strings into term-frequency/inverse-document-frequency vectors, reducing the influence of common terms and emphasizing more distinctive terms (scikit-learn developers, n.d.-a).
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Similarity Ranking\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 Cosine similarity compares the selected movie vector with every catalog vector; larger values indicate more similar text representations (scikit-learn developers, n.d.-b). The selected title is excluded, an optional genre filter is applied, and the remaining titles are sorted by score. The displayed percentage is a formatted similarity value, not a probability, review score, or guarantee of preference.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Design Rationale\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 Content-based filtering works without a rating history and offers a simple explanation: shared genres, creators, cast, and story terms contribute to similarity. Its weakness is dependence on metadata quality and coverage. Text overlap is only a proxy for audience preference.
\page
\pard\qc\b\sl480\slmult1 Implementation and Evaluation\b0\par
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Application Architecture\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 The project separates presentation from ranking. app.py loads data, defines controls, searches the catalog, and renders cards. recommender.py validates inputs, builds text features, vectorizes them, calculates similarities, and returns ranked rows. This separation supports focused tests. requirements.txt lists pandas, scikit-learn, and Streamlit.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Setup and Operation\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 The README explains how to create a Python environment, install requirements, and launch the app with streamlit run app.py. Streamlit prints a local address, usually http://localhost:8501. The catalog path is relative to the app file. Installation needs network access, but the app does not download movie records at runtime.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Tests and Quality Checks\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 tests/test_recommender.py checks that a similar film ranks above an unrelated one, genre filtering works, and unknown titles raise an error. These tests do not evaluate the interface, accessibility, browser support, or recommendation quality. Broader evaluation needs a larger verified catalog and relevance judgments, for example to calculate precision at k.
\pard\sb240\sa0\sl480\slmult1\b Limitations and Future Work\b0\par
\pard\fi720\sl480\slmult1 The small catalog limits variety, generic text can inflate similarity, and the system has no ratings or viewing signals. TF-IDF is recomputed for each request, which is inefficient at larger scale. Posters rely on an external image service. Future work could use a larger licensed dataset, validate records and image links, cache vectors, gather voluntary feedback with consent, and compare content-based, collaborative, and hybrid methods. Any collected data should be minimized and protected.
